shattered-maps-core is the pure half of Shattered-Maps: the neutral map file (`MapFile` v1), the identity and link-evidence rules, the room content baseline, slices, the neutral export, an SVG renderer and the strict validator. Reference and diagrams: docs/shattered-maps/schema.md. It runs unchanged in Node (copilot-server world memory, importers) and in the browser (the Maps viewer), so nothing here may touch the file system, the network, a timer or the clock; callers inject them (ids from a random source, dates as strings), and the tsconfig sets `types: []` to enforce it.

Unlike copilot-core this package is NOT local-only: production map surfaces will import it, so it must never import `@shatteredarchive/copilot-core` or carry COPILOT_BUNDLE_MARKER (scripts/check-local-only.mjs would then flag a production bundle).

Every operation returns a changed copy (or null when it cannot be done) and never modifies its argument; tests assert that. A new field on the file needs three edits that must agree: the type in schema.ts, the strict reader in validate.ts (unknown fields are errors), and the docs page. `src/testing/` is test-only and excluded from the build in tsconfig.json. `src/export/merc.ts` is the Builder export (docs/shattered-maps/builder-export.md) and the one place this package depends on another workspace package, `@shatteredarchive/merc-area` (pure too, so the browser build is unchanged); a jest config that loads the core (this one, copilot-server, shattered-maps-client) needs the `^@shatteredarchive/merc-area$` mapper to its src. `src/import/` is the community-map import (docs/shattered-maps/import-azah.md): its tests use `node:sqlite` for a fixture database, but the shipped code takes plain rows and a structural database handle, so it still has no Node dependency; `scripts/import-azah.mjs` is the only code that opens a file.
 `src/publish/` turns the audited map file into the per-area slices and manifest the C# site serves (docs/shattered-maps/world-map-api.md): pure like the rest, with `scripts/publish-audited.mjs` as the only code that gzips, hashes and writes files.

 `src/verify/` checks the community map against play: `trace.ts` follows a walk on Azah's graph (the method the map build uses), `verify.ts` compares a layer of walked rooms with Azah, `azahIndex.ts` is the shared matcher. Findings, numbers and the lessons for Copilot are in docs/shattered-maps/verification.md; `scripts/verify-azah.mjs` is the layer comparison's command line and refuses any world but dsl (Azah mapped the DSL only).

 `src/layout.ts` places rooms that have no position by following exits (the renderer's grid: N is y-1, E is x+1, U/D change z); used for maps built from walks (the Simulacrum's), never to move a placed room. Worlds (docs/shattered-maps/worlds.md): this package is production-bound and names no world other than the DSL's; the Simulacrum's table is in copilot-server.

 `src/mine/layer.ts` is the player's own layer (Phase 40): a map file of rooms a player added, filed under a continent/area/subzone (an existing id or a new name), and `mergeLayer`, which lays it over one published area without changing the published map. Pure; the caller gives the date and the random source. A layer belongs to ONE world (the caller keeps that); the file has no world field on purpose. The client keeps the layer in IndexedDB (apps/game-client features/worldmap/mine).

 `src/fog.ts` (Phase 37): `applyFog(map, visible)` is the fog of war for any viewer: a copy holding only the visible rooms, with an exit towards an unseen room turned into the unknown stub (`?`) and `conflictTo` dropped (it may name unseen rooms). It never reads who visited what: the caller gives the set. Used by the game-client map window; the C# page and the mobile screen could use it the same way.

continentLayout.ts (Phase 45) lays a continent's area boxes out without overlap from a spec of compass relations (the spec names area ids and lives with the owner's data, never here); kinds.ts proposes maze subzones from the exits (it never changes a map) and applies the ones the owner confirmed. render.ts draws a link as a full line only when classifyLink says it makes a clean join, otherwise a partial line ending in a diamond or an hourglass, and draws maze/loop/teleport/tangled rooms as coloured hexagons: change classifyLink's thresholds together with kinds.ts (it counts the same exits) and with the legends (Mapper SliceView, the C# page WorldMapV2.cshtml, MapHelp.tsx) and the user guide.
terrainPalette.ts (the colours, the only terrain code the browser bundle carries) and terrain.ts (mining the sector out of GMCP room_data, applying it to a map) are split on purpose: the bundle recipe in scripts/publish-map-renderer.mjs lists terrainPalette.js, not terrain.js. continentLayout.ts also takes an `outline` (the real silhouette, any units) and `hints` (area -> position in those units): it runs the plain layout first for the relative directions, then stretches that picture over the outline row by row and packs the boxes inside it (layoutInside); the outline only shapes, it never changes which area is west of which. autoLayout.ts derives a layout spec for a continent from the links between its areas (the owner's own spec always wins).
render.ts has one tile shape, the octagon (the game has eight compass directions plus up and down); RenderView.layout is only the literal 'grid'. A saved 'hex' from an older viewer is ignored by the renderer, not an error.
